  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: 500mb Cyclonic low pressure over the eastern \nseaboard with subsequent shortwave ridging upstream over the Great \nLakes Region begins to break down today and progress eastward. At \nthe same time upstream troughing with attached surface low pressure \ncontinues to develop in the central U.S and expand moisture through \nthe Midwest by early Wednesday morning. Light rainfall will persist \nacross the CWA this Wednesday and Thursday as a slow-moving frontal \nboundary works across the region.\n\nCooler air will settle into the Northwoods this weekend as surface \nhigh pressure builds across Northern Ontario and pushes cool, dry \nnortherly air into Northern Michigan. Temperatures are expected to \nremain near climatological normal for early October with highs in \nthe 60s and overnight lows around 40. Quiet conditions are expected \nto persist through the remainder of the forecast period as midlevel \nzonal flow keeps any energy to our north. \n\nForecast Details: \n\nToday and tonight: Subsidence aloft will continue dry and quiet \nconditions today as summer-like temperatures build into the 70s \nacross the CWA. No active weather to report other than the morning \ndense fog expected to quickly burn off/mix out as the surface \ninversion breaks down. The majority of the day is expected to be \nmostly sunny to partly cloudy with some low-level cumulus clouds \nthrough midday before burning off and being replaced by mid/high-\nlevel stratus this evening from the incoming frontal wave. \n\nWednesday and Thursday: Couple rounds of light precipitation are \nexpected to return after a prolonged dry spell to Northern Michigan. \nEven with poor midlevel dynamics and a boundary dissipating with \ntime, southerly moisture advection will produce appreciable rainfall \namounts this Wednesday and Thursday. Highest amounts favor \nsouthwestern parts of the CWA, with moderate chances of an inch or \nmore, but the majority of Northern Lower will likely observe around \na half inch of QPF over the course of 36-48 hours. Again, rainfall \nintensity is expected to be low, but after a couple of weeks with no \nobserved precipitation, this rainfall will be beneficial for a dry \nfall season.\n\nFriday through Monday Outlook: Overall quiet and dry weather is \nexpected to persist through the remainder of the forecast period as \nhigh pressure builds across Northwestern Ontario. Little to no \nchances are expected this weekend into early next week as zonal \n500mb flow punts any atmospheric disturbances north of the CWA. Low-\nend PoPs will remain in the forecast for this Sunday as long-range \nguidance depicts showers expanding south and clipping Eastern Upper \nand the Tip of the Mitt, but confidence remains low regarding any \nobservable rainfall as mid and low levels appear very dry. Otherwise \ntemperatures cool slightly compared to today and return to \nseasonable norms for October with highs in the 60s and overnight \nlows around 40.",
